\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The Laplacian Moore-Penrose inverse}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{With the [kernel decomposition](fdip-0031), define the
\newvocab{Laplacian pseudoinverse} by
##{L^+=iL_H^{-1}p_H.}
This is the unique endomorphism #{A} satisfying the four
\newvocab{Moore-Penrose identities}:
##{\begin{aligned}
LAL&=L,& ALA&=A,\\
(LA)^\dagger&=LA,&(AL)^\dagger&=AL.
\end{aligned}}
It vanishes on #{K}, is the inverse of #{L_H} on #{H}, and has spectral
expression #{L^+=\sum_{\lambda_a>0}\lambda_a^{-1}P_{u_a}}, where
#{P_u x=h(u,x)u} for a unit eigenvector.}
\proof{
\p{On the orthogonal decomposition #{K\mathbin\perp H}, the operators
#{L,L^+} have blocks #{(0,L_H)} and #{(0,L_H^{-1})}. Hence
##{LL^+=L^+L=P_H.}
The inverse on #{H} is self-adjoint by the
[inverse-response lemma](fdip-001Q). Thus #{L^+} is self-adjoint, both
products are self-adjoint, and multiplication once more gives
#{LL^+L=L} and #{L^+LL^+=L^+}.}
\p{Suppose #{A} satisfies the four identities. Then #{LA} is
self-adjoint and idempotent, since #{LALA=LA}. Its range lies in
#{\operatorname{ran}L=H}, and #{LAL=L} shows it acts as the identity
on #{H}. Thus it is the orthogonal projection #{P_H}.
Similarly #{AL} is self-adjoint and idempotent. Its kernel is #{K}:
#{Lx=0} implies #{ALx=0}, while #{ALx=0} implies
#{Lx=LALx=0}. A self-adjoint idempotent is the orthogonal projection
onto its kernel complement, so #{AL=P_H}. For #{y=Lx\in H},
##{Ay=ALx=P_Hx=L_H^{-1}y.}
Finally #{A=ALA=A(LA)=AP_H}, so #{A} vanishes on #{K}.
These two restrictions force #{A=L^+}. The argument includes #{H=0}
and #{E=0}; the unique map on the zero space is its own inverse.}
}
\p{TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{LinearMap.IsMoorePenroseInverse}
uses exactly these four conditions for real inner-product spaces.
Its \leanref/tauceti{LinearMap.IsMoorePenroseInverse.unique}
supports uniqueness, and the finite-dimensional characterization
\leanref/tauceti{LinearMap.isMoorePenroseInverse_iff_eq_moorePenroseInverse}
identifies this construction with #{L.\mathrm{moorePenroseInverse}}.
The weaker inner-inverse identity #{LAL=L} alone gives neither this
uniqueness nor a constrained probability law.}
\p{The construction is a direct specialization of the orthogonal spectral
decomposition in \citet{Section A.5.2, pp. 646-647}{boyd2004convex}.
The four-identity and uniqueness arguments above establish the additional
Moore-Penrose conclusion; no Gaussian law follows from that conclusion alone.}
